--------------------------- 1. Public -------------------------------
Use public when you want everyone to use it, even code from another project.

---- Code :-
public class Calculator
{
    public int Add(int a, int b)
    {
        return a + b;
    }
}

Now any project can do
---- Code :-
Calculator calc = new Calculator();
calc.Add(10, 20);

When should we use it?

Use public for:
APIs
Services
Controllers
Methods other developers should call
DTOs
Models that need to be shared

----------------------------- 2. Private ------------------------------------
Use private when something is only for the class itself.

---- Code :-
public class BankAccount
{
    private decimal balance;

    public void Deposit(decimal amount)
    {
        balance += amount;
        LogTransaction();
    }

    private void LogTransaction()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Transaction logged");
    }
}

Outside the class:

---- Code :-
BankAccount account = new BankAccount();

account.Deposit(100);      // ✅

account.LogTransaction();  // ❌ Error

account.balance;           // ❌ Error


----------------------------- 3. Internal -------------------------------

This one confuses many beginners.

Suppose you have:
Solution
│
├── PaymentAPI
│
├── PaymentCore
│
└── ConsoleApp

PaymentCore is one project (assembly).
If something is

---- Code :-
internal class PaymentValidator
{
}

then
Inside PaymentCore :-
- Service
- Repository
- Manager
- Controller

can all use it.

But from another project: ConsoleApp
you cannot.
PaymentCore  ✅
ConsoleApp   ❌
WebAPI       ❌